[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Memory Management and Caching in NoSQL Databases"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Efficient memory management and caching are critical components of high-performance NoSQL databases. Because NoSQL systems often handle massive datasets that exceed available RAM, employing intelligent eviction and caching algorithms like Least Recently Used (LRU) and Least Frequently Used (LFU) ensures optimal throughput and low latency."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Role of Caching in NoSQL Architecture"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"NoSQL databases rely heavily on in-memory caching to reduce disk I/O operations. When a read or write request occurs, the database engine checks the memory buffer pool before hitting persistent storage. When the cache is full, eviction policies must decide which items to discard to make room for new data."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Least Recently Used (LRU) Eviction Policy"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"LRU discards the least recently used items first. This algorithm assumes that data accessed recently will likely be accessed again soon. It is highly effective for temporal locality workloads."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"How it works: Tracks the time of last access for every key in the cache."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Simple to implement, works well for general-purpose caching."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Vulnerable to cache pollution during batch scans (one-time heavy queries)."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Least Frequently Used (LFU) Eviction Policy"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"LFU tracks how often an item is needed, evicting the items with the lowest access frequency. It prioritizes items based on popularity rather than recency."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"How it works: Maintains a frequency counter for each cached key."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Excels at retaining items that have steady, high-frequency access patterns over time."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Struggles with historical data whose popularity drops suddenly, requiring frequency decay mechanisms."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation Example: LRU Cache in Node.js"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is a conceptual implementation of an LRU cache typically used in application-level NoSQL caching layers using a Map data structure."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"class LRUCache {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.cache = new Map();
  }

  get(key) {
    if (!this.cache.has(key)) return -1;
    const value = this.cache.get(key);
    this.cache.delete(key);
    this.cache.set(key, value);
    return value;
  }

  put(key, value) {
    if (this.cache.has(key)) {
      this.cache.delete(key);
    } else if (this.cache.size >= this.capacity) {
      const oldestKey = this.cache.keys().next().value;
      this.cache.delete(oldestKey);
    }
    this.cache.set(key, value);
  }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for NoSQL Memory Tuning"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Monitor eviction rates: High eviction rates under normal load indicate insufficient RAM or poor cache sizing."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Match workload to policy: Use LRU for session stores and temporal data, and LFU for static reference catalogs or frequent user profiles."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Set proper TTLs: Combine LRU/LFU with Time-To-Live (TTL) expirations to prevent stale data from lingering in memory."}]
